  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nThe upper flow flattens out over the next several days as the \nsouthern ridge expands eastward and shortwave energy ejects out of a \ndeepening Pacific northwest trough.  After quieter late week \nweather, warm air advection with increasing PWATs will bring \nunsettled weather back into the Great Lakes.  Several shortwaves \nwill ride along a quasi-stationary boundary layed out across the \narea, resulting in several rounds of precipitation potential. \nTemperatures will be on the way up, with above normal readings \nlikely over the weekend into next week as the thermal ridge expands \nnorth and east.  \n\nAn isolated shower possible closer to Lake Huron through early this \nevening.  That activity should dissipate fairly quickly, with a good \nradiational cooling night setting up with clearing skies and light \nwinds.  Many interior locations will dip into the 40s (50s \nlakeshore) with even a few upper 30s possible in the typical colder \nlocations.  Areas of fog also likely in low lying areas. Quiet \nweather Friday as high pressure slides across the area with lots of \nsunshine and temperatures generally in the 70s. \n\nQuestion marks increase as we head into the weekend as developing \nwarm air advection brings deeper moisture into the Great Lakes.\nThis will eventually bring PWATs of 200% of normal to the \nnorthern Great Lakes with rounds of showers/storms moving across\nthe area. Saturday remains questionable as to how dry (or wet) \nit will be, as some models hold back the deeper moisture until \nSaturday night and Sunday. The current thinking will have lower \nrain chances during the day on Saturday, increasing Saturday \nnight into Sunday. As shortwave energy ejects out of the \ndeepening western trough and moves eastward into the Great Lakes\nin fast upper flow, expect several rounds of shower/storms \nchances for later in the weekend into the first part of next \nweek. This has some classic signatures for heavy rain potential \nwith an 850mb front layed out across the area, near parallel \nupper level flow to a quasi-stationary surface boundary, and \ndeep moisture. Much of northern Michigan sits in marginal \nexcessive rain potential for Sunday and Monday. Despite the rain\nchances, temperatures will trend above normal through this time\nas the thermal ridge expands north and east.",
